# scripts/pipeline/required-rows -- the rows a landing requires (PROTOCOL 6): landed = on GitHub main AND
# the standing journey run at that sha has every line below PASS, or UNCONFIGURED for an input the box
# DECLARES absent ($PIPELINE_ROOT/absent-inputs.tsv). Source: MATURATION-MAP-20261009 section 3 (DEPLOYABLE-1).
#
# One results.tsv id per line (column 1 of $JOURNEY_ROOT/TIP/results.tsv). A row id covers the row AND
# every sub-row ROW/*; a parent row that is UNCONFIGURED only because of a declared sub-row is accepted.
# A required id with NO line in results.tsv is MISSING and fails the landing (a row cannot pass by vanishing).
# landing.sh reads this file from the MAIN BASE's tree (the policy in force), never from the candidate's:
# a lane cannot drop its own required rows. `pending NAME note` lines are required rows that do not exist
# yet; landing.sh prints them on every landing and does not count them (they are DEPLOYABLE-1 work).
# A pending row FLIPS TO REQUIRED AUTOMATICALLY the first time results.tsv has a line with its id (NAME or
# NAME/*): from then on it must PASS like any other line here (decision: ROOT, 2026-10-14).
# `pending NAME when-green-at-main note` (ROOT, 2026-10-14, births): the row exists and runs on every landing, but
# flips to required only once main's own baseline run has it PASS (every line NAME, NAME/*). Until then landing.sh
# prints its status and does not count it; the baseline-relative rule (b) still applies to it like any line.
#
# BASELINE-RELATIVE RULE (ROOT 2026-10-14; reds named, never ledgered: docs/COORDINATION-2026-10-27.md §2, 2026-10-27).
# A candidate lands when:
#   (a) its static gate is red=0, AND
#   (b) its standing run on this box has NO line non-PASS (or missing) that was PASS in main's own most recent
#       standing run on the same box ($ARTIFACT_ROOT/main -> $JOURNEY_ROOT/<main>/results.tsv), AND
#   (c) every required line is PASS, or declared absent by name ($PIPELINE_ROOT/absent-inputs.tsv), or was ALREADY
#       non-PASS at main's baseline: such a line prints as PRE-RED by name and blocks nothing. There is no list of
#       accepted reds to maintain: the run's output names them, and a lane's brief names which one it owns.
# landing.sh prints "new reds vs baseline: N" and "required unmet: [...] (of which pre-existing at main: [...])";
# exit 0 iff new reds = 0 and nothing required is unmet beyond the pre-existing ones.
